<DocLayout
  title="Providers & models"
  description="Routers and presets, local Ollama models, fallback chains, Thinking controls, deep search, and embeddings in SAARTHI-STUDIO."
>
  <p>
    Studio is model-agnostic: it speaks the OpenAI-compatible API, so any
    endpoint that speaks it can power chat and agents. You choose where
    requests go; nothing is sent anywhere else.
  </p>

  <h2>Routers and presets</h2>
  <p>
    Settings ships presets for twelve OpenAI-compatible platforms —
    FreeLLMAPI, OmniRoute, Hugging Face, Groq, Cerebras, Gemini, OpenAI,
    OpenRouter, Mistral, Cloudflare, Together, and Novita. Pick a preset,
    paste the endpoint's API key, and test the connection.
  </p>
  <p>
    With FreeLLMAPI, Studio can also manage the router's lifecycle: it
    starts the router when it is stopped and stops it on exit
    <em>only if Studio started it</em>. A router you launched yourself
    keeps running. The embedded router console exposes providers, the
    model catalog, routing strategy, analytics, and logs over loopback.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Provider keys are typed into the router, never stored by Studio.
    Studio keeps only the router's unified key, server-side, in a
    permission-restricted configuration file.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Local models (Ollama)</h2>
  <p>
    The Local AI panel detects your hardware, lists local models, and
    pulls new ones with progress. A local model needs no router key and
    no network: chat, context, and history all stay on the machine.
    Local models also serve embeddings for semantic index search when
    you prefix the embedding model with <code>local:</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Fallback and health</h2>
  <p>
    Fallback chains, circuit breaking, and provider health are owned by
    the router and depend on available provider quota — free tiers are
    not unlimited. Studio surfaces provider health and the usage/error
    log with latency and token counts when the provider reports them.
    Price and context-window figures are shown only when a provider
    publishes them.
  </p>

  <h2>Thinking and deep search</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Thinking.</strong> On providers that support reasoning
      controls, the composer's Think chip selects the effort level; the
      default follows the model.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deep search.</strong> A composer toggle that researches the
      question with planned web queries (Exa/Tavily) or a provider's
      built-in web tools, streams each step, and replies with cited
      sources.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Context budget</h2>
  <p>
    Every request is sized like a coding agent's: a hard per-request
    ceiling (6k tokens by default), a names-only project outline, old
    attachments replaced by one-line markers, and head/tail cropping so
    a long thread never resends itself. The composer shows a live
    <code>~used / budget</code> meter. Media attachments are folded into
    the request only for the newest user message, so history never
    re-sends stale images or audio.
  </p>

  <h2>Embeddings</h2>
  <p>
    Semantic search over the codebase index is optional and stays off
    until you set an embedding model — either a router model or a
    <code>local:</code> Ollama model. Word search always works without
    one. See <a href={withBase('/docs/workspace/')}>Workspace &amp; IDE</a>.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
